<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Servatius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 384</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Servatius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Servatius was a 4th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the Tungri, with his see at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. He took part in church councils, including that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> in 343, and opposed the Arian doctrine. According to tradition he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> in 384, where his tomb became the centre of a major pilgrimage, and the see moved there and later to Liège. He is venerated as a saint, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/servatius.jpg" alt="Servatius" width="320" height="283" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kleon3 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maastricht,_Treasury_of_Saint_Servatius,_reliquary_bust_of_Servatius2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
